#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17b31ef7-a3d0-5315-8dbd-e92eafcce17f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Differential Equation Using Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "c25573a9-f51a-5cf2-aa32-a343e96dd732" :question/math-academy-id "e-6612" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/6e4e0c7cab964302651798fb741f1144f92f6e2e57e65101a7943111932fdbbd.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation has roots ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/a397e1a4149f4b6081f65d95f04741e60e2bd31fca19d3f8e09ed0f76db581de.png) so the complementary solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/c970482f98369bea187d9d95494395b7ddc3777c96c6f70928f5f7b2aae5f3b7.png)\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/d0cdd6ff1b44946ac1e910d23f7f40b83908992acbdba94329de63b7ee2c8f49.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/7ffc37c1e99b8efa7829e449730a07f729b1089965446233266d7b7b26d0ce90.png) is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/9f015e4ef6033cdc7ac1ff0de31ac36bf8d47b6fa702b773c32abc955bdb0d2f.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/6709a006285d5cd34ad5d0cc38c5a301ee5e685991d0a63609d33e3dc9273524.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/b4c9f2e8e219faee88048f605f5ecf0957d84ed21896bbee9b25e7cb2953364d.png) are constants to be determined.\n\n**Caution:** The terms of the complementary solution are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/c1e8f4a9b683824e14a5b29e1bb1f23a90c03f90929c1ac8d04a3f2ba78d4c74.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/373d49e91c81adc100005e789dec85074254a9692d7858313966c886b589ec58.png) Although ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/16099b9989ef712e435b858d14cb006c5e62362412ae1b0e42e8025290dc644c.png) appears, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/669043a118b0a018e699c203330af94918b1df9744a2d9c6e04109e4ca39499a.png) is not itself a term because it is multiplied by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/6b761e20295ebe1f6a5edf87ae5b7e480485dafbc0e623f4fab18efe2b8859c6.png)\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/6316c498377eadd30922f7f5ef6cfa1015cb3c059349bccf2aec7ca52ea5356e.png) we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/48db0d80886317e03b0a772e8f36255308c9e1f8bac5824231981366a39e96f5.png)\n\nTo find the values of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/6053d6cf8a3a4f931034631411cd2b07f16470ea835ae768c5783b33e0d18d55.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/f00a9053a63ce88c1749e8ce7fb389b3618c6e3d4be6a4a03b7031759fe62940.png) we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/e43bebbc4a12d780e880c4aa2fbb6f09f74e388163648858c95b0f2969d9168d.png)\n\nThe corresponding terms on both sides should have the same coefficients. Equating the coefficients of like terms, we get the following system:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/5f5871fd2c97ac62a9143268e844d0b0b0482ef93e8841138b5d0e6d08bf911f.png)\n\nSolving this system gives ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/bb5b3d4d7afe88b271c5feb2d48e9a5680c728cf6f33396eb4f226ad9bc4f3ff.png)\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/03049680a7f1934e4eb0a7031d73baf4789ee549f4495c563739dfe91ad1d19a.png)\nand the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065513/assets/6d839822be46648100d639e231ca1b4afe9460d4dec7b6a17c761f25475be381.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"}] :question/id #uuid "b11bf2d1-c79a-5308-bcda-109d217b14cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6613" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 4y = 4\\sin 2x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation has roots $\\lambda = \\pm 2 \\textrm{i},$ so the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos{2x} + B \\sin{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $4 \\sin 2x,$ and $\\sin 2x$ is a term in the complementary solution. So, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = x (\\alpha \\cos{2x} + \\beta \\sin{2x}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x), \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+4x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+4x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ -4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ -4\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -1$ and $\\beta = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = -x \\cos{2x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087554 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c07e202-40b7-5508-af3d-6a272ed0cbc2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "a23b7e25-1876-5a71-a638-18b5d548bd16" :question/math-academy-id "e-6788" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 4y = 4\\sin 2x\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x) = A \\cos{2x} + B \\sin{2x} - x \\cos{2x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 4y = 4\\sin 2x, \\qquad y(0) = 2 , \\ \\ y'(0) = -3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\ 2 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-2A\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2B\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =-2A\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2B\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y'(0)=-3$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =-2A\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2B\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\ -3 & =2B-1 \\\\ -2 & =2B \\\\ -1 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=-1,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c07e202-40b7-5508-af3d-6a272ed0cbc2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94083d65-4a98-503f-99f6-7d2c346de080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\cos 3x - \\sin 3x + x\\cos 3x + x\\sin 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085934} {:answer/id #uuid "97cb78d7-bbcb-59fb-843a-2ed2d7833ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2\\cos 3x + \\sin 3x + 2x\\cos 3x + x\\sin 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085935} {:answer/id #uuid "a1534329-1fba-515c-ae3c-f11f561e3ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\cos 3x - 4\\sin 3x + x\\cos 3x + 5x\\sin 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085936} {:answer/id #uuid "a62fa116-32ac-5f1b-bf99-f912975f5d54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\cos 3x - 2\\sin 3x + x\\cos 3x - x\\sin 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085937} {:answer/id #uuid "a92cc537-cd9a-546c-9bb3-325b63bf005f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2\\cos 3x + 3\\sin 3x + 4x\\cos 3x + x\\sin 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97cb78d7-bbcb-59fb-843a-2ed2d7833ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2\\cos 3x + \\sin 3x + 2x\\cos 3x + x\\sin 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085935} :answer-field/id #uuid "976aa527-321c-5b0c-a9d3-b13424b4db15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a70227b-6237-55b5-b378-b7662770487e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64228" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm 3\\text{i},$ and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+3x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ {y}^{″} & =6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -6\\alpha =-12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3P\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+x(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3Q+2=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P=2,Q=1.$\n\nSo, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17b31ef7-a3d0-5315-8dbd-e92eafcce17f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Differential Equation Using Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e1c4d24-619f-5051-82a2-511b397ab796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350507} {:answer/id #uuid "a563cd84-b0a6-5a5f-ad34-f043a356dfd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350508} {:answer/id #uuid "041e228e-177c-5179-aec8-62d95bb5d0a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350509} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f16f47-2a92-5c07-8d92-22855a9a9855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350510} {:answer/id #uuid "25cd2478-e1cb-5caa-9f7f-6d77c4bf5328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3f16f47-2a92-5c07-8d92-22855a9a9855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350510} :answer-field/id #uuid "965f3522-d077-51f2-9601-9261f4ea3e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40251d29-1129-53ff-869d-37eb1dd17a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-64272" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ So we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =0$ and $\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91b3f5a8-3b2c-5755-bee0-fc30a055f06d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = x\\cos 3x - x\\sin 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085946} {:answer/id #uuid "bb060350-7550-5537-a3e7-466c038bf858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-x\\sin 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085947} {:answer/id #uuid "1985efb7-2303-5654-9dc1-16a2e0f7f9d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = x\\cos 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085948} {:answer/id #uuid "60c2ee01-0268-55e7-95cd-0cdfe667451a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = 2x\\cos 3x + 5x\\sin 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085949} {:answer/id #uuid "76f7d593-532b-5f91-9f36-273628a8394f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = x\\cos 3x + x\\sin 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb060350-7550-5537-a3e7-466c038bf858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-x\\sin 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085947} :answer-field/id #uuid "af993e0a-d970-5d1c-abe2-ed48d257b3e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bd36cb5-e3b7-5d05-9be7-a0cde384d7ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-64275" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+9y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+3x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)-9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)-9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -6\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =0$ and $\\beta =-1.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a67cf121-1598-5905-be54-66bfd7ada383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350514} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce7ffa8-5f9d-5052-ba4d-36c6d3e3cc22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350515} {:answer/id #uuid "79954736-b91a-5a10-a77f-9b484c6e7db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350516} {:answer/id #uuid "d75cc318-bbd3-5fb6-b3e4-01dade5f43df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350517} {:answer/id #uuid "456ecdf5-a640-5037-9cc0-edebaf985069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d75cc318-bbd3-5fb6-b3e4-01dade5f43df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350517} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e1d6a2f-bcea-59ab-9410-fce0fcdaa4a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15e8beb3-32f1-5089-9d6d-90628aa946bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64278" :question/problem "Find the general solutionto the problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+4x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & =8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+16x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\,=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-8\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+8\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8\\beta =16\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ -8\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1$ and $\\beta =2.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)=-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c07e202-40b7-5508-af3d-6a272ed0cbc2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2da94e-5d83-5cc4-b922-5da484863e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{-5x}+3{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350521} {:answer/id #uuid "01661c86-3cb2-50dc-b414-9498512ac756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{-5x}-3{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350522} {:answer/id #uuid "6f395c76-0fc0-5e2d-a0a3-9f66ba25b7fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5x}+\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350523} {:answer/id #uuid "978821da-1f00-5673-8db8-54fd13e341dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-5x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350524} {:answer/id #uuid "4c78b423-4134-5f0f-8c21-4334a2d23e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c78b423-4134-5f0f-8c21-4334a2d23e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350525} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d8df641-6d7d-5631-809e-5e897f2b82c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71d9f52c-cfa8-5732-ac53-ee44eea5c603" :question/math-academy-id "q-8110" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-5y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\\,y(0)=-\\frac{2}{5},\\,{y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-5y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-5$ or $\\lambda =1$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)-5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n(-14\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(-14\\beta -12\\alpha )\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-14\\alpha +12\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -14\\beta -12\\alpha =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{10},\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-\\frac{2}{5}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{10}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{10}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-5A+B=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -5A+B=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0,$ $B=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17b31ef7-a3d0-5315-8dbd-e92eafcce17f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Differential Equation Using Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a46c4865-f0b4-5d4f-ba37-ce7f3fdc2cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-3x} + Be^{x} + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086953} {:answer/id #uuid "db697b2c-c9ac-5bc3-b80b-2a871635ab86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{3x} + Be^{x} - \\frac{2}{3}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086954} {:answer/id #uuid "01e28f97-dbbb-5016-ba6a-f2101b558239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{3x} + Be^{-x} - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086955} {:answer/id #uuid "15dc77c3-d245-53bf-9be4-e8bdadfc9e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-3x} + Be^{x} - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086956} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba31070-c1a8-53d0-b7e3-4fc7617bac7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-3x} + Be^{x} - \\frac{3}{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15dc77c3-d245-53bf-9be4-e8bdadfc9e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-3x} + Be^{x} - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086956} :answer-field/id #uuid "e121f7c9-9eb9-5224-ae85-c6aeba971611" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57e5b668-9411-5b21-b6d0-177014c608f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-8112" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =1$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta -4\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-2\\alpha -4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -4\\beta -2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},$ $\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17b31ef7-a3d0-5315-8dbd-e92eafcce17f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Differential Equation Using Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a971620-e112-5416-92a6-bec8eba6071f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350528} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf16bb6-b431-5487-88c0-26d8fde108ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350529} {:answer/id #uuid "a73c1008-86ac-5db0-a9ba-2b58b7ded68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350530} {:answer/id #uuid "beda6e04-8e88-5ff0-89dd-be506e3f3150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350531} {:answer/id #uuid "5a514275-b21a-5b89-a6d9-cd4be67a2398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "beda6e04-8e88-5ff0-89dd-be506e3f3150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350531} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a829b9e-23fc-537f-8bb4-8fea2f42cc28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e0ea13a-5920-5140-b5e8-72fe8bddf332" :question/math-academy-id "q-8113" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{4}=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{3}{4}\\alpha -\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(\\alpha -\\frac{3}{4}\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}\\alpha -\\beta =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\alpha -\\frac{3}{4}\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-12$ and $\\beta =-16.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c07e202-40b7-5508-af3d-6a272ed0cbc2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93ae5d1f-0c82-5e69-b9fb-884004b20507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 4e^{-x} + 2\\cos 2x + \\sin 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086959} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f28f9a-fbcb-5d19-b12a-c77331fd5a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-6xe^{-x} + 4\\cos 2x + 3\\sin 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086960} {:answer/id #uuid "39a5d010-3a69-5be3-8e02-f0d15c8f9f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 6e^{-x} - e^{x} + 4\\cos 2x + 3\\sin 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086961} {:answer/id #uuid "625d16c2-668f-5d31-a20f-579d15ccf8eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2e^{-x} + 6xe^{-x} + 2\\cos 2x + \\sin 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086962} {:answer/id #uuid "652e2d5b-328f-5552-b801-60bcf167bd8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 6e^{-x} + 12xe^{-x} + 4\\cos 2x + 3\\sin 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2f28f9a-fbcb-5d19-b12a-c77331fd5a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-6xe^{-x} + 4\\cos 2x + 3\\sin 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086960} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c8445a6-c3e9-50f6-b421-320a32dbd4d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f684abde-2923-5a1b-8719-a76da5c1d85c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8114" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,\\,y(0)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda +1)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(-4\\alpha -3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ -4\\alpha -3\\beta =-25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =4$ and $\\beta =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4 & =4 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-x}-Bx{e}^{-x}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-A+B+6 & =0 \\\\ B & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=0,B=-6.$\n\nSo, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-6x{e}^{-x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17b31ef7-a3d0-5315-8dbd-e92eafcce17f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Differential Equation Using Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c027b638-ed18-5413-800a-d6bf65070c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+\\frac{12}{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{5}{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350535} {:answer/id #uuid "4887874e-6e0a-5024-8588-6b15912527e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}-14\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350536} {:answer/id #uuid "49e7c27f-32de-5658-9d91-3b1ba2504f45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350537} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e34c89-bf3b-5b3c-a71b-535081ea1e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350538} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef6dcfa-0d28-57ad-b327-a0e441a4fc27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}-\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8e34c89-bf3b-5b3c-a71b-535081ea1e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350538} :answer-field/id #uuid "65993043-cbf7-51c5-8e29-e9582a8a6d28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8f1d0ee-caed-5870-b7f3-2b9f001fcee2" :question/math-academy-id "q-8115" :question/problem "Find the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -2)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =2$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $13\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)-4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-5\\alpha -12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(12\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha -12\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ 12\\alpha -5\\beta =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{12}{13}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{5}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17b31ef7-a3d0-5315-8dbd-e92eafcce17f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Differential Equation Using Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f079b9-ba65-54a8-b37d-7104574b9097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350542} {:answer/id #uuid "b48ab56f-1a11-55f3-adec-80f762dfea88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350543} {:answer/id #uuid "d21b9228-e57c-59dc-a248-7d58e6231b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350544} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab84f8c-c16e-5354-91f3-9ae634f6f23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350545} {:answer/id #uuid "a69c8afe-c690-534e-83ef-fc8aca1fa2f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}+3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ab84f8c-c16e-5354-91f3-9ae634f6f23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350545} :answer-field/id #uuid "cedbb40c-afe1-5c32-97b9-5da1e20d6ab9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "899267d3-b838-5ee1-a670-9eb248328799" :question/math-academy-id "q-8116" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+10y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+31\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+31\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+2(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+31\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(-6\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+31\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +6\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -6\\alpha +\\beta =31\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-5$ and $\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c69ed520-591c-5eb8-9b04-ef260c4a0ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = P\\cos x + Q\\sin x + \\frac{1}{4}\\cos 2x + \\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086965} {:answer/id #uuid "434012ab-92d4-5613-be0e-1ed247361611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = P\\cos 2x + Q\\sin 2x + x(\\frac{1}{4}\\cos 2x + 2\\sin 2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086966} {:answer/id #uuid "467d453b-5d76-5d50-9207-1196c6f97ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = P\\cos 2x + Q\\sin 2x + x(\\frac{3}{2}\\cos 2x - \\sin 2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086967} {:answer/id #uuid "567702f8-94c7-5fae-91a1-b83adf26eda8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = P\\cos 2x + Q\\sin 2x + x(\\frac{3}{4}\\cos 2x + \\sin 2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086968} {:answer/id #uuid "7435bc69-736c-556c-8478-6ab331cd8479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = P\\cos x + Q\\sin x + \\cos 2x - \\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "434012ab-92d4-5613-be0e-1ed247361611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = P\\cos 2x + Q\\sin 2x + x(\\frac{1}{4}\\cos 2x + 2\\sin 2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086966} :answer-field/id #uuid "262057f1-1f1b-5961-be7a-00ac7f916685" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fa3488c-5c68-5a75-a2a4-028f5f72f615" :question/math-academy-id "q-8120" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the  equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4y=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{2i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$ \n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ -4\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38d7e289-2cb2-5180-8216-e0d4ee8dbf39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350549} {:answer/id #uuid "c28d9e0f-fe39-599a-8c3d-3615f3e606b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350550} {:answer/id #uuid "f02c676f-9856-5912-9c63-94cce81655d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350551} {:answer/id #uuid "6f47d809-3dc8-5975-997f-902958cd74d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350552} {:answer/id #uuid "65242a86-d76b-5198-b76c-c625c65e3382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c28d9e0f-fe39-599a-8c3d-3615f3e606b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350550} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce61187a-c4e5-5eaf-8d42-50628b181564" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe1794bb-4b33-510b-8172-929bfa639d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-8854" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+4x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+16x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\,=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-8\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+8\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ -8\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2}$, $\\beta =0.$\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49058657-4d12-5a36-82da-a795008076ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350556} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5b8f4e-e156-58f5-809c-4536a709ee0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350557} {:answer/id #uuid "2d61145b-ab99-5392-ac4b-a95caf6547ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350558} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9ec605-636f-51d2-984a-e0a271212594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350559} {:answer/id #uuid "55091203-2f38-5281-a8e5-db99d9b0868d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d61145b-ab99-5392-ac4b-a95caf6547ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350558} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1137cbd-b541-51d8-a51b-f095b15f2f22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37b3f55e-ca0e-5e9e-ab06-288798980949" :question/math-academy-id "q-93823" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+25y=-20\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x,$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+5x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =10(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x)-25x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n10(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x)-25x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+25x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)=-20\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-10\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+10\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x=-20\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}10\\beta =-20\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x) \\\\ -10\\alpha =-10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =-2.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c07e202-40b7-5508-af3d-6a272ed0cbc2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c684499-2b9e-5795-95fc-818b6d3c9276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350563} {:answer/id #uuid "be630e47-1661-50f4-ba43-76de0fc4ea22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350564} {:answer/id #uuid "9a71bd3e-fd74-50b6-a395-2725f4d2b3fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5x{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350565} {:answer/id #uuid "b17fb142-9594-53ea-9697-8435fc47aae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350566} {:answer/id #uuid "935ee502-27c3-5987-a825-e207fa3daffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c684499-2b9e-5795-95fc-818b6d3c9276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350563} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c0b8c3f-136c-5384-b7fb-cbd95d454ff7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0162100e-48be-5a9b-85a0-6b9a2747d5ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-93829" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=-25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=-25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =A{e}^{2(0)}+B(0){e}^{2(0)}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ -3 & =A-3 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2A{e}^{2x}+2Bx{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =2A{e}^{2(0)}+2B(0){e}^{2(0)}+B{e}^{2(0)}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 5 & =2A+B+4 \\\\ 5 & =2(0)+B+4 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=0$ and $B=1,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny=x{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c07e202-40b7-5508-af3d-6a272ed0cbc2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9fd276b-ccaa-527f-b905-3984898d44f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350570} {:answer/id #uuid "85d019aa-1c4a-5062-a879-21655fb4a4b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}-4x{e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350571} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef2894c-5dc2-5e0a-9918-ca0dcf42cf77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350572} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f389f0-34d9-5e37-ae87-0d6788fa54fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350573} {:answer/id #uuid "1e79d889-4211-53b1-9bcc-f8f8b4d956dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-2x}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3f389f0-34d9-5e37-ae87-0d6788fa54fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350573} :answer-field/id #uuid "80be25af-b993-5a9c-9034-51cb4c90b000" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f37c31e4-e252-5030-a6f5-72637debeb9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-93833" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,\\,y(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =A{e}^{-2(0)}+B(0){e}^{-2(0)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(0) \\\\ -1 & =A-2 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}-2Bx{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-2$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =-2A{e}^{-2(0)}-2B(0){e}^{-2(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2(0) \\\\ -2 & =-2A+B \\\\ -2 & =-2+B \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=1$ and $B=0,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$"}]}
